You are a senior Flutter engineer. We are building "Souqi" (سوقي), an offline point-of-sale and invoice app for small shops in Syria: grocery stores, internet-card / subscription renewal shops, or any shop that sells products, services or cards. The app must stay GENERIC — nothing specific to one shop is hard-coded; everything shop-specific is a setting. This is the permanent project brief. Follow it in every task; I will send the work phase by phase.

PLATFORM & STACK
- Android only (phones and tablets). Flutter stable, Dart 3, null-safety, Material 3.
- 100% offline. No server, no internet, no accounts, no analytics. All data in a local SQLite database (sqflite).
- State management: flutter_riverpod. Keep a clean layered structure: data (db, repositories) / domain (models) / features (screens, providers).
- Android package id: com.souqi.souqi (project name: souqi).
- Google Maven is blocked on the developer's network: Aliyun mirrors (maven.aliyun.com) are configured in android/settings.gradle.kts, android/build.gradle.kts and the user's ~/.gradle/init.gradle. Never remove them.

LANGUAGES
- Full Arabic and English UI using flutter_localizations + ARB files (app_ar.arb, app_en.arb). No hard-coded user-facing strings anywhere.
- Arabic is RTL, English is LTR. Layout must mirror correctly.
- App language and INVOICE language are two separate settings (e.g. Arabic UI, English invoice). Default: Arabic for both.
- Use a font with good Arabic support (e.g. Cairo or Tajawal via bundled asset, not network).

CORE FEATURES (later phases)
1. Products: name, optional short code (e.g. "101"), kind, unit, price, favorite flag, active flag. Fast search by name or code. Kind is either GOODS (sold by quantity and unit, e.g. 0.5 kg sugar) or SERVICE/CARD (sold per piece, quantity 1 by default, no unit needed, e.g. "Internet 100 GB package" or "ADSL 4 Mbps monthly renewal"); a SERVICE/CARD product can be marked "requires customer account" so a sale of it must be linked to a customer's card/account number.
2. Units: preset list (kg, g, bag, piece, box, liter) + user-defined units. Quantities can be decimals (0.5 kg).
3. Sale screen ("type-to-sell", built after field feedback — shops do not set up products in advance): add items by tapping favorites, searching, or typing a code; set quantity; live total; save + print/share invoice.
   - Typing a name with no exact match shows "+ Add «text»" on top (also above partial matches); Enter opens it. Digits-only text with no matching code shows "No item with code …" instead (typos and unknown barcodes never become products).
   - The new item opens in the quantity sheet (price field focused, qty 1, last-used unit, GOODS, base currency, "More" for unit/kind/currency, "Don't save as a product" checkbox).
   - New products are created only when the invoice is SAVED, inside the invoice transaction; abandoned carts create nothing. A name matching an existing (or deactivated) product reuses it, using the shared Arabic name normalization (leading "ال" per word, أ/إ/آ→ا, ة→ه, ى→ي, no tatweel/diacritics).
   - A changed unit price (product's own currency, > 0) is written back to the product on invoice save (setting, default on).
   - Favorites bar: starred products first, then the top sellers of the last 30 days by number of sales (up to 12 automatic; setting to turn off). Search ranking: exact code, exact name, starts-with, contains; best sellers first within each.
   - Settings → Selling holds these three switches (save new items, update prices from sales, automatic favorites), all on by default — and a fourth, "cash received field", OFF by default: a small line under the total that opens a dialog and shows the change to give back (lib/features/sale/cash_received.dart). It is a line that opens a dialog, not a text field in the panel: the keyboard collapses the totals panel, so a field inside it would vanish when focused. The amount is never stored.
4. Invoice settings (fully dynamic, stored in DB): paper width (80 mm default, 58 mm, or custom mm), printer DPI (203 default), side margins, base font size, shop name, logo, header text, footer text, invoice language, and toggles for which fields to show (item code, unit, unit price, date/time, invoice number).
5. Printing to a Bluetooth ESC/POS thermal printer. Arabic text must print correctly, so the invoice is rendered as a Flutter widget, captured to an image at the exact printable width in dots, converted to monochrome and sent as an ESC/POS raster image. Shops without a printer are fully supported: output mode Print / Share / Both; share the invoice as an image (default) or PDF, send it straight to the customer's WhatsApp chat when the customer has a phone number, or save it to the phone.
6. Invoice archive: search by date range / invoice number / product, view, reprint, delete one, delete a date range, and auto-delete invoices older than N days (user setting, off by default).
7. Statistics: sales total, invoice count, average invoice, top products by quantity and by amount, for today / this week / this month / custom range, plus comparison with the previous period.
8. Extras: bulk price change by percentage (all or selected products, with rounding option), barcode scan with camera, discount per invoice (amount or %), favorites bar, data backup/restore to a single file, PIN lock for settings/archive/stats.
9. Debt ledger — built in phase 4d (partial payments, receipts, statements, debtors list, opening balances), behind `debtEnabled`, licensable in 5b.
10. Multiple currencies (fully offline, rates entered manually by the shop owner):
   - Currencies table: code, Arabic name, English name, symbol, decimal places, exchange rate to the base currency, active flag, sort order. Seed SYP (base, 0 decimals) and USD (2 decimals); the user can add any currency (e.g. TRY, EUR, or "old lira" for the Syrian redenomination) and edit/disable them.
   - One BASE currency (default SYP) used for totals and statistics. The base currency can only be changed while there are no invoices and no debt transactions; after that the setting is locked with an explanation (no recalculation logic).
   - Each product has its own price currency (e.g. sugar priced in SYP, a carton of oil priced in USD). On the sale screen every line is converted to the invoice currency using the current rate.
   - Per sale the user can choose the invoice currency (default = base). The invoice can optionally show the total in up to 2 extra currencies (e.g. "Total: 400 SYP ≈ 0.04 USD"), configurable in invoice settings.
   - Quick "update exchange rate" action reachable from the sale screen in two taps, showing when the rate was last updated.
   - Every invoice stores a SNAPSHOT of the currency, the rate(s) used and each line's original price + original currency, so changing rates never changes old invoices.
11. Customers, cards/accounts & renewals (for internet-card and subscription shops, usable by any shop):
   - Customers: name, phone (optional), notes. Shared with the debt ledger (one customers table).
   - Accounts: a customer can have several cards/accounts. Each has: account/card number (text, keep leading zeros), label/type (e.g. "internet card", "ADSL line"), provider (free text with suggestions), default package (a SERVICE/CARD product), renewal period in days (optional), last renewal date, next due date, notes, active flag.
   - One quick search that matches customer name, phone or card/account number (partial, ignoring spaces and dashes), with a one-tap "Renew" that opens a sale with the customer, account and default package ready.
   - A "Due for renewal" list (overdue / today / next N days).
   - Invoices store snapshots of customer name, phone and account number; the invoice can print them (with an option to mask all but the last 4 digits).
12. Prepaid card codes (scratch/voucher cards): a SERVICE/CARD product can be marked "sold from code stock". The shop owner loads codes (secret PIN + optional serial + optional expiry) in batches; selling N cards automatically takes N available codes (earliest expiry first, then oldest batch), marks them sold, links them to the invoice (and customer if attached) and prints them on the invoice. Codes are treated like cash: encrypted at rest, masked on screen, revealed only with the PIN, and every sensitive action is logged.
13. Offline licensing: each device needs an activation code signed with the developer's Ed25519 private key (the app holds only the public key). The license carries the shop name and which features are on; there is a trial period; without a valid license the shop's data stays readable and exportable but selling is blocked. Built in phase 5b.

FEATURE FLAGS
- lib/config/features.dart with `const bool debtEnabled = true; const bool barcodeEnabled = true; const bool discountEnabled = true; const bool customersEnabled = true; const bool accountsEnabled = true; const bool voucherStockEnabled = true;` (accounts and debt both require customersEnabled). Debt UI never reads `debtEnabled` directly: every debt screen, button, invoice line and statistic asks `debtFeatureProvider` (lib/core/providers.dart) — the one switch phase 5b ties to the license.
- Also a runtime setting "shop type" (Grocery / Cards & subscriptions / Both) chosen on first launch and changeable in settings. It only changes defaults and what is shown first (e.g. Cards shops open on the quick customer search and the due-for-renewal list; Grocery shops open on the favorites sale screen). It never deletes data.
- When a flag is false, nothing related to it appears anywhere (menus, buttons, invoice lines, stats). The DB tables still exist from version 1 so enabling later needs no migration.

DATA RULES (important)
- Money: store as INTEGER minor units of its currency (SYP with 0 decimals = whole pounds, USD with 2 decimals = cents), always together with its currency code. Quantities: store as INTEGER thousandths (500 = 0.5) to avoid floating-point errors. Line total = round(price * qty / 1000).
- Exchange rates: never use double for stored rates. Store each rate as an exact fraction of two INTEGERs (rate_num, rate_den, both > 0) meaning "rate_num/rate_den base units per 1 unit of this currency", exactly as the user entered it (e.g. "1 USD = 13000 SYP" → 13000/1). Every rate snapshot (invoices, extra totals, invoice items, debt transactions) uses the same num/den pair. Do conversions with BigInt math and one explicit rounding step (half-up) at the end, to the target currency's decimal places. Put all money/currency math in one tested module.
- Invoice items store a SNAPSHOT of product name, code, kind, unit and unit price at sale time, so later price changes never alter old invoices. Invoices also store a snapshot of the linked customer (name, phone) and account (number, provider), with nullable foreign keys to the live records.
- Phone and card/account numbers: store as text exactly as entered, plus a normalized search column with an index. Phones: digits only (Arabic-Indic digits ٠-٩ converted to 0-9). Account numbers and voucher codes are ALPHANUMERIC: trim, remove spaces and dashes, convert Arabic-Indic digits, uppercase letters — never strip letters.
- Customers are never hard-deleted (soft delete via active flag); foreign keys from accounts and debt transactions to customers use ON DELETE RESTRICT.
- Invoice numbers are sequential from a persisted counter and never reuse numbers, even after deletion.
- Card codes: tables voucher_batches (product, added date, count, cost per card optional, note) and voucher_codes (product, batch, encrypted code, code hash, serial, expiry, status AVAILABLE / SOLD / VOID, status_changed_at, sold_at, invoice id, invoice item id, plus sale snapshots that survive invoice deletion: sold_invoice_no, sold_customer_id (no FK), sold_customer_name, sold_account_number). Products have a nullable low_stock_threshold. Encrypt the code with AES-GCM using a random key kept in flutter_secure_storage (Android Keystore); store a SHA-256 hash of the normalized code (alphanumeric normalization above) with a UNIQUE index per product to block duplicates and allow lookup. Taking codes for a sale happens in the SAME transaction as saving the invoice, so two sales can never get the same code. A table audit_log records reveal / reprint-with-codes / void / restore / import / export actions with time and details (never the code itself).
- Use versioned migrations (onCreate + onUpgrade with explicit version steps). Never drop user data in a migration.
- Wrap multi-step writes (saving an invoice + its items) in transactions.

QUALITY
- Run `flutter analyze` with zero warnings and write unit tests for money/quantity math, migrations and repositories. If you can run commands, run them; if not, tell me the exact commands.
- Give me complete files, not fragments, and tell me every file you changed.
- At the end of every phase, list exactly what I should test on a real device.

SECRETS
- Never create, request, store or commit the licensing PRIVATE key inside this project. Only the public key may appear in the app.

BUILD NOTES
- Keep `kotlin.incremental=false` in android/gradle.properties (fixes a Windows Kotlin cache lock bug). Never remove it.
- UI imports come from package:material_ui (Flutter 3.50); localization delegates from material_ui's GlobalMaterialLocalizations.delegates.
- Bluetooth printing uses our own platform channel (PrinterConnection.kt) and our own ESC/POS raster code (lib/core/printing/escpos.dart); do not add printer plugins.
- Database is at schema version 5 (see lib/data/db/migrations for what each step did). Every schema change is a new version step with a migration test that upgrades a populated older database without data loss.
- Do not run `flutter test` and `flutter build`/`run` at the same time (l10n files get regenerated); `flutter gen-l10n` restores them.
- Phase 4b (customers, accounts, renewals, due list) is done. Renewal = an invoice with an account attached that sells at least one SERVICE/CARD item; it updates last renewal / next due in the invoice transaction.
- Phase 4c (prepaid card codes, PIN, audit log) is done. Codes are taken in `VoucherRepository.sellForItem`, called inside `InvoiceRepository.save`'s transaction. `code_hash` is HMAC-SHA-256 (keyed with a second random key kept next to the AES key in flutter_secure_storage), not a bare SHA-256: a bare hash of a numeric PIN can be brute-forced from a copy of the database. Both keys never leave the Android Keystore, so a restored database alone cannot read its codes (see PHASE 5 NOTES). `requirePin(context)` in lib/features/security/pin_dialogs.dart is the gate for every sensitive action; phase 5's lock for settings / archive / stats should reuse it. A plaintext code may only exist in: the load-codes input, `InvoiceCode.code` on its way to the printer / share file, and the "show the code" dialog.
- mobile_scanner uses the BUNDLED ML Kit model (offline, no Google Play services); never set `dev.steenbakker.mobile_scanner.useUnbundled`.
- This machine runs out of memory when several test files run at once: use `flutter test --concurrency=1` if the test shell dies with "Out of memory" / "Could not start thread DartWorker".
- PIN recovery: setting or changing the PIN shows a one-time RECOVERY CODE (12 characters, Crockford base32, shown as XXXX-XXXX-XXXX) once, with Print / Share / Save and an "I saved it" checkbox; only its salted hash is stored. "Forgot PIN?" on the PIN dialog takes that code, sets a new PIN and issues a new recovery code (the old one stops working). Wrong recovery attempts have their own counter with the same lock-out rules as the PIN. A PIN that has no recovery code (chosen on a phase 4c build) gets a banner in Settings → Security until one is created, and a one-time offer on the next PIN prompt; both confirm the PIN and then show the code (`PinRepository.issueRecoveryCode`). "Forgot PIN?" sits beside the PIN dialog's title, in the part that does not scroll. All in `PinRepository` (lib/data/repositories/pin_repository.dart) and lib/features/security/.
- Phase 4d (customer debt ledger, schema version 5) is done. All of it lives in lib/domain/models/debt.dart, lib/data/repositories/debt_repository.dart and lib/features/debts/ (plus lib/features/sale/payment_sheet.dart).
  - THE LEDGER: `debt_transactions` was rebuilt in version 5. Every entry has a type (DEBT from a sale, MANUAL_DEBT, PAYMENT, OPENING_BALANCE, ADJUSTMENT, REVERSAL), a `direction` (+1 = the customer owes more, -1 = owes less), `amount_base_minor` > 0, the original amount + currency + rate fraction when it was not the base currency, payment method, due date, receipt number (counter `receipt_no`, never reused), `invoice_id` + the `invoice_no` snapshot, `reverses_id`, `created_at` (the date it counts from in the account — may be in the past) and `recorded_at`. A balance is ALWAYS `SUM(amount_base_minor * direction)` and is never stored: positive = the customer owes the shop ("عليه"), negative = the shop owes the customer ("له").
  - ENTRIES ARE NEVER EDITED OR DELETED, and the database itself refuses: trigger `trg_debt_no_delete` aborts every delete, `trg_debt_no_update` aborts every update except the foreign key clearing `invoice_id` when an invoice is deleted. A correction is `DebtRepository.reverse` (a REVERSAL entry + an audit line, one transaction). Never add code that updates or deletes a ledger row, and never drop these triggers.
  - SAVE IS ONE TAP, ALWAYS: the main Save button saves the sale as paid in full at once (`SalePayment(paidMinor: total)`), with no sheet — a cash sale must never cost a second tap. The secondary "Credit / Partial" button beside it (`sale-credit`, only with the ledger on) opens `PaymentSheet` (it opens on "On credit"; "Partial" and "Paid in full" with cash received / change are one chip away). Never route Save through a sheet or a dialog again.
  - A SALE writes to the ledger only what was NOT paid: `InvoiceRepository.save(draft, payment: SalePayment)` calls `DebtRepository.planSale` / `postSale` inside the invoice transaction and posts one DEBT entry of `total - paid`. The invoice row keeps what it prints: `paid_minor`, `credit_used_minor`, `prev_balance_base_minor`, `new_balance_base_minor` (all NULL on invoices saved without the payment sheet, which print as before). "Use credit balance" is the same DEBT entry absorbing a negative balance; `credit_used_minor` only says so on the invoice. The credit limit is enforced there too (`CreditLimitExceededException` rolls the sale back unless `overrideCreditLimit`).
  - FIFO (payments pay off the oldest debits first) has ONE definition: `fifoUnpaid` / `summarizeAccount` in debt.dart, used for one account and for all of them. The all-customers snapshot is `DebtRepository.accountsQuery`: one grouped forward pass over the covering `idx_debt_fifo` that hands each customer's debits to Dart packed in one string. Do not "simplify" it into window functions — the SQL must run on sqlite 3.9 (Android 7): no window functions (3.25), no row values, no FILTER, no UPSERT. Measured at 200,000 entries: a correlated sum per debit took 2.3 s, a recursive walk 0.6 s, this pass 0.25 s.
  - WHAT THE PIN OPENS LOCKS AGAIN (lib/features/security/pin_session.dart): after the "lock again after" setting (1 / 5 / 15 minutes, default 5) without a touch, and AT ONCE when the app goes to the background (`hidden` / `paused` / `detached` — not `inactive`, which is also a permission dialog). `PinSessionObserver` sits above every screen in `SouqiApp`. The debts are one shared session (`debtUnlockedProvider`, a `PinSessionNotifier` that owns the idle timer); EVERY debt screen's body is inside a `DebtLockGate`, which counts touches, and keeps its child alive but hidden while locked so a half-typed form survives the PIN; sheets opened from a debt screen use `ClosesWithDebtSession`. A single screen or dialog opened with the PIN (audit log, card lookup, a revealed card code) is wrapped in `PinGuardedRoute` and closes itself by the same two rules. Anything new that the PIN opens and leaves on screen gets one of the two; never an unlock that lasts for the run of the app.
  - THE ONE EXCEPTION TO "AT ONCE IN THE BACKGROUND": when the APP ITSELF put something in front of itself (share sheet, WhatsApp, dialer, file picker, photo picker, a system settings page), the seller has 2 minutes (`PinAutoLock.externalActionGrace`) to come back without the PIN. The home button, another app or the screen off still lock at once, and the idle time keeps running meanwhile (with "lock again after 1 minute", 70 seconds in WhatsApp is the PIN again). How it works: `PinBackgroundGuard` (pin_session.dart) is told BEFORE the platform call (`externalActionStarting`), and treats the next trip to the background as that action's — one announcement, one trip; an announcement not followed by the app losing the focus within 10 seconds, or answered "nothing opened", is dropped. The announcing is done in ONE place: `ExternalActionBridge` (lib/core/platform/external_action_bridge.dart) wraps the real bridge inside `nativeBridgeProvider` — so screens keep using `nativeBridgeProvider`, never `platformBridgeProvider`, and a NEW bridge method that opens another app must go through `_external` there. The only external launch outside the bridge is the logo photo picker, which announces by hand; any new plugin that opens another app must do the same. Elapsed time is checked against `PinClock` when the app comes back (wall clock AND a running clock), not only by timers: Android freezes background apps, and the wall clock can be set back. Tests: test/features/pin_session_test.dart; a test that wants the announcements overrides `platformBridgeProvider` with the fake bridge, not `nativeBridgeProvider`.
  - THE PIN: reversing an entry and adjusting a balance go through `requirePin` and are logged (`DEBT_REVERSAL`, `DEBT_ADJUSTMENT`); a sale over the credit limit asks for the PIN when one is set, a plain confirmation when none is, and is logged either way (`CREDIT_LIMIT_OVERRIDE`). The optional "PIN to open debts" setting is `requireDebtAccess` / `DebtLockGate`; recording a debt at a sale never asks.
  - PAPERS: `ReceiptView` and `StatementView` (debt_papers.dart) go through `LedgerPaperOutput` (debt_output.dart), the same capture → raster / PNG / PDF pipeline as `InvoiceView`; a receipt opened again from the ledger is a COPY.
  - After ANY write to the ledger call `refreshDebtData(container)`. Deleting invoices (one, a range, retention) never touches ledger rows beyond the `invoice_id` link; `idx_debt_invoice` is what keeps those deletes fast.
- Phase 4e (daily automatic debt report) is done; no schema change (still version 5). Settings → Debts → "Daily debt report" (lib/features/debts/report/). The app still NEVER sends anything by itself: files land in a folder, and only the user's tap on "Share" hands them to another app.
  - THE JOB is `DailyReportJob` (daily_job.dart): build every `DailyOutput`, stage its files in `<app files>/reports/` (those copies are what the notification's Share sends), copy them to the destination, apply each output's keep-N, record the status, post the notification. The SAME code runs in the app (catch-up on start, "Create report now") and in the background engine. It only READS the database; its status (last attempt / success / outcome / problem / files) lives on the Android side (SharedPreferences via `ReportPlatform.saveStatus`), so two engines never write the database at once. One run at a time across both engines (`ReportRunLock`, stale after 10 minutes).
  - SCHEDULING: our own channel, no plugin (like the printer). `ReportScheduler.kt` enqueues ONE one-time WorkManager request (`androidx.work:work-runtime`, through the Aliyun mirrors) for the next occurrence of the chosen local time; `DebtReportWorker.kt` starts a headless FlutterEngine on `debtReportWorkerMain` (lib/main.dart, `@pragma('vm:entry-point')`), waits for "workerFinished", then APPENDS the next run (APPEND_OR_REPLACE — REPLACE from inside the worker would cancel itself) and always returns success. Not a periodic request (drifts, and a time-zone change would move it for good). No exact alarms. WorkManager keeps it across reboots/updates; every app start also calls `schedule()` without replace, which re-plans only if the plan is missing or no longer matches the clock.
  - CATCH-UP RULE (`needsCatchUp`, report_schedule.dart): on app start, if the last SUCCESSFUL report is older than the last due time — worked out on the wall clock where the phone is now, compared as instants — a report is made now. Nothing is owed from before `enabledAt`; a failed attempt is retried after 10 minutes, not on every start. A fallback to Downloads counts as success.
  - DESTINATION: a Storage Access Framework tree picked with ACTION_OPEN_DOCUMENT_TREE (persisted read+write grant), or Download/Souqi/Reports via MediaStore (Android 10+; Android 9 and older write the file and need WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE, asked when the report is turned on). If the folder's grant is gone or its storage does not answer — or a write fails half-way — the WHOLE set goes to Downloads, the status says "fallback", and a notification explains it even with "Notify me" off. A file of the same name is overwritten (`souqi-debts-YYYY-MM-DD.xlsx/.pdf`, one set per day). Keep-N counts DAYS THAT HAVE A REPORT (`DatedFilePattern.namesToDelete`), so a phone that was off for weeks never loses its reports to the calendar, and only files matching an output's own pattern are ever deleted.
  - CONTENT (`DebtReportRepository`, read-only): the same `DebtRepository.debtors` snapshot and `periodQuery` as the debtors list and statistics, so the report cannot disagree with the screens. "Today's movements" = entries DATED or WRITTEN today. "All balances" = every customer with at least one ledger entry. Invoice language decides the report language (RTL sheets for Arabic); the notification is in the app language. Excel is our own pure-Dart writer (lib/core/reports/xlsx_writer.dart, `archive` for the zip): numeric money cells in the base currency's format, date cells, inline strings, frozen header row, fitted widths — never through a double. The PDF is vector text with the bundled Tajawal (pdf package Arabic shaping; columns reversed by hand for RTL, Table has no RTL), and `initializeDateFormatting` is called first because the background engine has no Material localizations.
  - PRIVACY / PIN: turning the report on and changing its folder (including back to Downloads) ask for the PIN when one is set (never forcing one) and are logged (`DEBT_REPORT_ON`, `DEBT_REPORT_OFF`, `DEBT_REPORT_FOLDER`). The notification is VISIBILITY_PRIVATE with a title-only public version. Every notification tap opens the APP (`ReportLaunch` → `takeLaunchAction`); Share and Open folder then go through `requireDebtAccess` and `nativeBridgeProvider` (`shareFiles`, `openFolder` — announced, so the external-action grace applies); the folder picker and the battery-optimization dialog are announced too.

PHASE 5 NOTES
- Licensing (phase 5b): the debt ledger becomes a licensable feature by making `debtFeatureProvider` also depend on the license; nothing else in the UI reads the `debtEnabled` constant. Turning it off hides every debt screen, invoice line and statistic and makes Save skip the payment sheet; the ledger rows stay in the database untouched. ONE exception to "only the provider": `debtReportWorkerMain` (lib/main.dart) runs in the background engine, which has no ProviderScope, and passes `debtEnabled && customersEnabled` as `featureOn` — 5b must add the license check THERE too, or an unlicensed phone keeps writing daily debt reports.
- Daily backup (phase 5): the encrypted BACKUP file is a second output of the SAME daily job — implement `DailyOutput` (lib/features/debts/report/daily_job.dart) as e.g. `BackupOutput` with its own `DatedFilePattern` (e.g. `souqi-backup-YYYY-MM-DD.<ext>`), its own keep-N setting and a `build()` that writes the encrypted file into `DailyJobContext.stagingDir`, and add it to `dailyOutputs()`. The scheduler, the worker, the destination folder, the fallback to Downloads, the run lock, the status and the notification need no change; keep-N never touches another output's files (tested with a fake backup output in test/data/debt_report_test.dart). If the backup should run with the debt report off, give the job its own "enabled" rule rather than reusing `DebtReportSettings.enabled`, and move the settings page out of Debts. The backup must never stage the card-code keys in the clear (see below).
- Backup / restore (phase 5): the backup file must carry BOTH card-code keys — the AES key that encrypts the codes and the HMAC key behind `code_hash` — re-encrypted with the backup password, and restore must write both back into flutter_secure_storage on the new phone. With only the AES key the codes decrypt but duplicate detection and "search by full code" silently stop matching; with neither, the codes are unreadable. Both live in one secure-storage entry (`CodeCipher.keyName`, 64 bytes: AES ‖ HMAC), so export and import that entry as a whole. Never write the keys into the backup in the clear, and never into the database.
- PIN reset by the developer (phase 5b): `SignedPinReset` in pin_repository.dart and `signedPinResetProvider` in lib/core/providers.dart are the hook. 5b provides an implementation that verifies a developer-signed, device-bound, single-use "PIN reset" code with the licensing PUBLIC key (Ed25519) and overrides the provider; the "Forgot PIN?" dialog then accepts that code next to the recovery code, and `PinRepository.resetWithSignedCode` sets the new PIN. Nothing else needs to change.
